faq:
  - q: "What are the dominant industries in the Chicago job market?"
    a: "Chicago is the US's third-largest business center and has a more diverse economy than any single-industry hub. Dominant sectors: (1) Financial Services and Trading — Chicago is the global center of derivatives trading and futures markets. CME Group, CBOE, and the Chicago Board of Trade anchor a dense ecosystem of trading firms (Jump Trading, DRW, Citadel, Virtu Financial, Jane Street Chicago). The trading firm culture is quantitative, performance-driven, and distinct from Wall Street banking. (2) Management Consulting — Chicago is the home market for several major consulting firms. McKinsey Chicago, BCG Chicago, Deloitte (Chicago is a major office), A.T. Kearney (founded in Chicago, now Kearney), and Oliver Wyman all have strong Chicago presences. (3) Healthcare — Northwestern Memorial, Rush University Medical Center, University of Chicago Medicine, and Advocate Aurora Health are among the largest employers in Illinois. The Chicago area is also a significant biotech hub. (4) Technology — Salesforce, Morningstar, Motorola Solutions, Orbitz (Expedia), and a growing tech startup ecosystem anchored by 1871 and MATTER accelerators. Amazon, Google, and Meta all have Chicago offices."
  - q: "How is Chicago's financial services market different from New York?"
    a: "Chicago's financial services market is fundamentally different from New York's: (1) Trading-dominant vs. banking-dominant — Chicago's financial strength is in derivatives trading, quant funds, and market-making, not investment banking and PE. The most prestigious Chicago finance employers (Jump Trading, DRW, Citadel) are quantitative trading firms, not banks. (2) Quantitative skills are prized above all else — Chicago finance firms care more about math, statistics, and programming ability than about deal experience or relationship skills. Top Chicago trading firms recruit from MIT, Caltech, and top math programs. (3) Compensation at top trading firms exceeds NYC banking — Citadel, Jump, and DRW pay among the highest compensation in any industry, anywhere. (4) Culture is more direct and less hierarchical — Chicago's business culture is known for directness and less formality than NYC or SF counterparts."
  - q: "How does consulting recruiting work in Chicago?"
    a: "Chicago is a top recruiting market for MBB and other management consulting firms. Key facts: (1) University of Chicago Booth School of Business, Northwestern Kellogg, and DePaul Kellstadt are the primary MBA feeder programs for Chicago consulting. (2) McKinsey, BCG, Bain, and other MBB firms have significant Chicago offices that recruit from these programs on campus. (3) Kearney (A.T. Kearney) is headquartered in Chicago — a unique positioning advantage for candidates interested in strategy consulting with a Chicago base. (4) Deloitte's US headquarters is in Chicago, making it a center of gravity for Big Four consulting recruiting in the Midwest. (5) Non-MBA consulting recruiting in Chicago (analyst and associate roles for undergraduates) is active but less structured than MBA recruiting — direct applications to Chicago offices and alumni networks are important."
  - q: "What is the Chicago tech startup ecosystem like?"
    a: "Chicago's tech ecosystem is growing but remains smaller than SF, NYC, and Seattle in absolute terms. Key characteristics: (1) 1871 — Chicago's flagship tech hub, located in the Merchandise Mart, houses hundreds of startups and is a significant networking venue for Chicago tech. (2) Series A–C is the primary stage — Chicago has a strong seed and Series A ecosystem but fewer late-stage unicorns than coastal markets. Notable Chicago tech companies: Tempus (health AI), Outcome Health (healthcare), Sprout Social, Braintree (PayPal), and Grubhub (Just Eat Takeaway). (3) Industry focus — Chicago tech leans toward FinTech, HealthTech, AgTech, and enterprise software — reflecting Chicago's industry strengths. (4) Compensation is below coastal tech markets — Chicago tech roles typically pay 15–25% below equivalent SF or NYC roles, partially offset by Illinois' lower cost of living (though Illinois does have a state income tax at a flat 4.95%)."
  - q: "What is the best career coaching strategy for Chicago job seekers?"
    a: "Chicago-specific career coaching should address: (1) Trading firm preparation — for candidates targeting quantitative trading roles at Citadel, Jump, DRW, Virtu, or similar firms, preparation requires strong mathematical and statistical foundations, probability and brain teaser practice, and programming proficiency (C++, Python). These interviews are unlike any other finance or tech interviews. (2) Consulting case preparation — for MBB and Chicago strategy consulting targets, case preparation should begin months before application deadlines at Booth and Kellogg. (3) Chicago networking — the Chicago commercial club, Executives' Club of Chicago, industry associations (Illinois Technology Association), and alumni associations are the primary professional networking channels in Chicago. In-person relationship building is more important in Chicago than LinkedIn-only outreach. (4) Illinois state tax awareness — Illinois has a flat 4.95% state income tax; factor this into after-tax offer comparisons when evaluating Chicago roles against no-income-tax states."
